#: conf.json:summary
msgid "A MoonBit library for defining linear programs and solving them with the two-phase simplex method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:1
msgid "core API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:3
msgid "The package at `src` is the whole public surface of linear-program. Its interface file is [`src/linear-program.mbti`](../../../src/linear-program.mbti). The coefficient type `V` is generic; the solver needs the traits listed in each signature, and `Double` satisfies all of them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:5
msgid "Matrices in this package are `@mutable.Matrix[V]` from the `mutable` package of [linear-algebra](https://luna-flow.github.io/en/linear-algebra/). Row 0 of a program matrix holds the objective coefficients, the other rows hold one equality constraint each, and the last column holds the right-hand sides."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:7
msgid "Variables"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:11
msgid "`Variable` is a named decision variable with a lower and an upper bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:23
msgid "`Variable::new(name, low, up)` creates a variable. Two variables are equal when their names are equal, and they are ordered by name. `Show` prints the name; `show_all` prints the name with its bounds, for example `x1 : [0, 100]`. `One::one()` is the unnamed variable that `Poly::from_var_array` uses for a constant term."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:25
msgid "The bounds are stored and printed, but the solver does not read them: every variable is treated as non-negative. `Lp::to_standard` names the variables it adds `y1`, `y2`, ..., so do not use these names for your own variables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:27
msgid "Linear expressions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:31
msgid "`Poly[V]` is a linear expression: a map from variables to non-zero coefficients, kept in the order of the variable names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:46
#: manual/api/core.md:77
#: manual/api/core.md:107
#: manual/api/core.md:139
msgid "Function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:46
#: manual/api/core.md:77
#: manual/api/core.md:107
#: manual/api/core.md:139
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:48
msgid "The empty expression."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:49
msgid "Pairs `coeffs[i]` with `vars[i]` and skips zero coefficients. A coefficient beyond the last variable becomes the constant term."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:50
msgid "Adds `coeff` to the coefficient of `var` and removes the term when the sum is zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:51
msgid "A copy that does not share storage with the original."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:52
msgid "The stored coefficients in the order of the variable names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:53
msgid "One coefficient per entry of `vars`, with zero for a variable that does not occur."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:55
msgid "`Show` prints an expression such as `3.5x1 + 1.5x2 + -2x3`, and `-poly` negates every coefficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:57
msgid "Objective functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:61
msgid "`Obj_func[V]` is an objective: a linear expression with the sense minimise or maximise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:75
msgid "`Obj_func::new(sense)` creates an objective with an empty expression. The sense is `\"min\"`, `\"Min\"`, `\"MIN\"`, `\"minimize\"` or `\"Minimize\"` for minimisation and `\"max\"`, `\"Max\"`, `\"MAX\"`, `\"maximize\"` or `\"Maximize\"` for maximisation; any other string aborts. `Obj_func::from_array(sense, coeffs, vars)` also sets the expression from coefficients, like `Poly::from_var_array`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:79
msgid "A new objective with the same sense and the expression `poly`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:80
msgid "Adds a term to the expression in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:81
msgid "`true` for a maximisation objective."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:82
msgid "The equivalent minimisation objective; a maximisation objective is negated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:83
msgid "The coefficients of `to_array`, padded with zeros to length `n + 1`, the width of a tableau row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:85
msgid "`Show` prints the sense and the expression, for example `max  3.5x1 + 1.5x2 + -2x3`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:87
msgid "Constraints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:91
msgid "`Constraint[V]` is the constraint set of a program: equalities `poly = b` and inequalities `poly <= b` or `poly >= b`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:105
msgid "`Constraint::from_array(eq_array~, ineq_array~, vars)` builds the set from rows of coefficients. An equality row is `(coeffs, b)`; an inequality row is `(coeffs, relation, b)` where `relation` is `\">=\"` or `\"<=\"`. Any other relation aborts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:109
msgid "The empty set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:110
msgid "Appends `poly = b`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:111
msgid "Appends `poly relation b`; `relation` should be `\">=\"` or `\"<=\"`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:112
msgid "Replaces the equality at the 1-based position `i`; aborts when `i` is out of range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:113
msgid "Replaces the inequality at the 1-based position `i`; aborts when `i` is out of range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:114
msgid "One row `[coefficients..., b]` per equality, with columns in the order of `vars`. Inequalities are left out, so call it on a program in standard form."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:116
msgid "`Show` prints the set after `s.t.`, one constraint per line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:118
msgid "Programs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:122
msgid "`Lp[V]` is a linear program: its variables, objective, constraints and the matrix built from them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:137
msgid "Every function returns a new program and leaves its argument unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:141
msgid "A program with objective `obj`, variables `vars` and no constraints."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:142
msgid "The program with its constraints replaced, as in `Constraint::from_array`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:143
msgid "The program with new objective coefficients and the same sense."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:144
msgid "A program from a matrix: row 0 without its last entry gives the objective, every other row `[a..., b]` gives the equality `a · x = b`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:145
msgid "The equivalent program in standard form: a minimisation objective, equality constraints only, and non-negative right-hand sides. Each inequality receives a slack or surplus variable `y1`, `y2`, ... appended to the variables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:146
msgid "The constraint coefficients $A$ of the program matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:147
msgid "Row 0 of the program matrix without its last entry."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:148
msgid "The right-hand sides $b$ of the program matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:150
msgid "The program matrix is built from the objective and the equality constraints, so the three accessors describe a program with inequalities only after `to_standard`. `Show` prints the objective, the constraints and every variable with its bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:152
msgid "Solving"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:156
msgid "`two_stage` solves a program in standard form with the two-phase simplex method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:162
msgid "Call it on the result of `to_standard`. It returns the values of all variables of the standard form, including the added `y` variables, and the optimal value of the standard form, which is always a minimisation. For a program that maximises, the maximum is the negation of the returned value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:164
msgid "The solver aborts when the program is infeasible (phase 1 ends with a non-zero artificial objective) and when it is unbounded. Each simplex run stops after 1000 iterations and prints a message if it has not converged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:168
msgid "`pivot` performs one Gauss–Jordan pivot of a tableau in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:174
msgid "`Lp::pivot(matrix, row, col)` divides row `row` by the pivot element and eliminates column `col` from every other row. It aborts when the position lies outside the matrix or the pivot element is zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:178
msgid "`simplex_iteration` runs the simplex method on a tableau in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:184
msgid "Row 0 of the tableau is the objective row and the last column holds the right-hand sides. Each iteration enters the column with the most negative objective coefficient and leaves the row chosen by the minimum ratio test. The run stops when no objective coefficient is negative or after `max_iterations` iterations (1000 by default), and aborts when the program is unbounded. With `debug=true` it prints every pivot and the tableau after it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:186
msgid "`Lp::phase_1` and `Lp::phase_2`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:188
msgid "`phase_1` and `phase_2` are the two halves of `two_stage`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:195
msgid "`Lp::phase_1(tableau, artificial_index)` takes a tableau extended with artificial variables, where `artificial_index[i]` is the column of the artificial variable of constraint `i`. It eliminates the artificial columns from the objective row, runs `simplex_iteration` and returns the tableau."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:197
msgid "`Lp::phase_2(vars, objective, tableau)` drops the artificial columns of the phase 1 tableau, restores the original `objective`, runs `simplex_iteration` again and returns the basic solution with the value in the objective row. It aborts when phase 1 did not reach zero, because the program then has no feasible solution."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:199
msgid "Numeric tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:203
msgid "`ApproximatelyZero` decides whether a value is zero for the purposes of the solver."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:214
msgid "An `Int` is zero only when it equals `0`; a `Double` is zero when its absolute value is below $10^{-15}$. `double_equal_to_zero(x)` calls `is_zero_eps(x)`."
msgstr ""

